    "salt": "34YPMB5jTaVSX2E4kOHqzA==",
    "text": "Epona was the Celtic goddess of the horse. Her name means horse or mare. The Celts passed her cult to the Roman legionnaires. They honored her as the cavalry goddess. Roman-Celtic sculptures show her seated on a horse. She sits among horses and foals, or she offers them feed. The Celts gave the horse deep reverence. Because of this respect, they avoided eating horseflesh. That taboo reached modern European cooking. People lifted it only for ritual sacrifice. Horses died in the midsummer rites. In Ireland the horse stood for the sovereignty goddess. The banais ríghe, the ritual marriage binding a king to the land goddess, made that link.",
